### One Superclass, Multiple Subclasses

**Hierarchical Inheritance** occurs when **multiple subclasses extend a single shared superclass**:

```text
           Animal (Superclass)
          /   |   \
        Dog  Cat  Bird (Sibling Subclasses)
```

Common behavior is defined once in `Animal`, while each subclass introduces specialized functionality.

---


```java
// Shared Superclass
class Notification {
    protected String recipient;

    public Notification(String recipient) {
        this.recipient = recipient;
    }

    public void logDispatch(String channel) {
        System.out.println("[AUDIT LOG] Dispatching " + channel + " to: " + recipient);
    }
}

// Sibling Subclass 1
class EmailNotification extends Notification {
    private String subject;

    public EmailNotification(String recipient, String subject) {
        super(recipient);
        this.subject = subject;
    }

    public void sendEmail(String body) {
        logDispatch("EMAIL");
        System.out.println("Subject: " + subject + " | Body: " + body);
    }
}

// Sibling Subclass 2
class SMSNotification extends Notification {
    public SMSNotification(String recipient) {
        super(recipient);
    }

    public void sendSMS(String text) {
        logDispatch("SMS");
        System.out.println("SMS text: " + text);
    }
}
```

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        EmailNotification email = new EmailNotification("user@example.com", "Password Reset");
        email.sendEmail("Click the link to reset your credentials.");

        System.out.println("----------------------------------------");

        SMSNotification sms = new SMSNotification("+1-555-0199");
        sms.sendSMS("Verification code: 829104");
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```text
[AUDIT LOG] Dispatching EMAIL to: user@example.com
Subject: Password Reset | Body: Click the link to reset your credentials.
----------------------------------------
[AUDIT LOG] Dispatching SMS to: +1-555-0199
SMS text: Verification code: 829104
```

---

### Sibling Isolation in Hierarchical Inheritance

Notice that sibling subclasses operate with complete **isolation** from each other:
```java
EmailNotification email = new EmailNotification("user@example.com", "Alert");
email.sendEmail("Test");

// email.sendSMS("Hello"); // COMPILE ERROR! Sibling isolation!
```
Even though both extend `Notification`, `EmailNotification` knows nothing about methods declared exclusively in `SMSNotification`.

